## Method
1. After approval, write `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh` inside this analysis directory. Use Python in the existing `gars-bio` environment, with pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib (noninteractive Agg backend). All input validation and computation run in the submitted job. Record package versions, input paths and SHA-256 hashes in the report. If required packages are unavailable, stop and report the missing dependency; do not install software.
2. Read the tab-separated count matrix using `gene_id` as the gene identifier; retain `gene_name` as annotation only. Read the CSV design and match sample columns exactly to unique `sample_id` values, ordering columns by design rows. Require unique, nonempty gene IDs, unique sample IDs, nonempty condition labels, finite nonnegative numeric counts, positive total counts per sample, and exact agreement of the sample sets. Fail with a diagnostic on mismatch rather than dropping samples or guessing labels. Fractional Salmon length-scaled counts are accepted without rounding.
3. Compute each sample's library size across all genes before filtering. Retain genes with counts per million (CPM) at least 1 in at least 3 samples, matching the smallest condition group in this design. Normalize retained counts to CPM using those original library sizes and transform as `log2(CPM + 1)`. This is an explicit exploratory library-size normalization; do not use TPM or differential-expression results. Record library sizes and retained gene counts.
4. Remove genes with zero variance across samples after transformation. Rank the remainder by sample variance (ddof=1), breaking ties by ascending gene ID, and select the top 500 genes or all remaining genes if fewer than 500. Report the actual selection size. Require at least two variable genes and three samples, and enough matrix rank for two nonzero principal components; otherwise stop with a diagnostic.
5. Transpose to samples by genes and run `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples - 1, n_selected_genes), svd_solver="full", whiten=False)`. Centre each gene across samples; do not scale genes to unit variance. Conditions are used only for colouring and annotations, never to fit PCA or choose genes. Do not adjust batches, remove outliers, exclude samples or perform hypothesis tests.
6. Plot PC1 versus PC2 using Matplotlib: untreated blue (#0072B2), treated orange (#D55E00), one labelled point per sample, a condition legend, and axis labels stating explained variance percentages. Export PNG at 300 dpi and PDF. Export all fitted PC scores with sample IDs and conditions, per-component explained variance and cumulative fractions, and selected gene IDs with their transformed variances and PC loadings.
7. Write a concise report documenting validation, transformation, gene selection, sample counts per condition, explained variance, provenance and parameters. Limit conclusions to the requested descriptive PCA; apparent separation is exploratory and does not establish a condition effect. Confirm exported sample rows agree with the design and all scores and variance fractions are finite before declaring success.

